{
  "rationale": "The throughput floor (>=1 MSPS) is essentially non-binding: even the slowest schedule here (iterative, N+3 cycles/result) needs only Fmax/(N+3) >= 1, i.e. ~30 MHz at N=30, which every family clears by a wide margin. So the binding constraint is accuracy (max_abs_err <= 2^-10, i.e. accuracy_bits >= 10) and the real objective is area (luts_plus_ffs). Accuracy depends only on (data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding) and is identical across families, so the front is decided purely by which family gives the least area for a given accuracy point. iterative reuses ONE datapath (one adder set + barrel shifters + FSM) so its area is nearly flat in N, whereas unrolled_k/pipelined_m replicate the datapath N times (fixed shifts, but N adder sets) and pipelined_m adds ceil(N/m) register stages. I therefore expect iterative to own the low-area corner and most of the front, and I weight it accordingly, while spending enough on pipelined_m (large m) and unrolled_k to confirm they never undercut iterative at any accuracy level and to fill the front. Ranges bracket the accuracy threshold: output LSB is 2^-(W-2) so W>=12 is needed for 2^-10, and residual angle error ~2^-N needs N~10-12, so W in 10..18 and N in 6..20 straddle the feasible boundary without wasting budget far below it. angle_guard and frac_guard are searched over their full registry ranges because they trade area for error cheaply and could shift the min-area feasible point.",
  "families": [
    {
      "why": "Primary bet: single shared datapath with barrel shifters, area nearly independent of N, so it should give the smallest luts_plus_ffs at every accuracy level and own the min-area corner the selection rule picks. Wide W/N ranges bracket the 2^-10 threshold so the search can find the cheapest feasible point and the accuracy/area trade-off above it.",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 10, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 6, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "family": "iterative",
      "budget_share": 0.55
    },
    {
      "why": "Closest competitor to iterative on area: large m keeps the number of register stages (ceil(N/m)) small while the combinational micro-rotations use cheap fixed shifts. Included to test whether it can beat iterative at any accuracy point and to cover the front; m restricted to 4..8 to stay in the low-area regime.",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 10, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 6, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 4, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "budget_share": 0.25
    },
    {
      "why": "k chained micro-rotations/cycle with a shared FSM: area grows with k, so it is unlikely to win the min-area corner, but small k (2..3) may be competitive at high accuracy and it helps confirm the front is iterative-dominated. Small share to avoid over-spending on a likely-dominated family.",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 10, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 6, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "budget_share": 0.20
    }
  ]
}
